      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Lifeboat stands as one of Alfred Hitchcock's most daring technical experiments - a single-location thriller that traps eight survivors in a lifeboat after their ship is torpedoed by a German U-boat. Like <a href=\"/movie/389\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"389\">12 Angry Men</a> (1957) and <a href=\"/movie/1580\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"1580\">Rope</a> (1948), it proves how much dramatic tension can be wrung from spatial constraint. Hitchcock transforms the cramped setting into a pressure cooker of conflicting personalities and moral dilemmas, particularly when the survivors rescue a German sailor from their sunken attacker. The film's claustrophobic atmosphere influenced later confined-space classics like <a href=\"/movie/387\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"387\">Das Boot</a> (1981) and <a href=\"/movie/32084\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"32084\">Lebanon</a> (2009)."
      },
