      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Lifeboat represents a unique entry in the WWII film canon, eschewing combat spectacle for psychological warfare. While contemporaries like <a href=\"/movie/289\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"289\">Casablanca</a> (1942) and <a href=\"/movie/27367\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"27367\">Mrs. Miniver</a> (1942) depicted the war's impact on society at large, Hitchcock distills the conflict down to a microcosm of Allied-Axis relations. This approach influenced later war films like <a href=\"/movie/15876\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"15876\">The Enemy Below</a> (1957) and <a href=\"/movie/17657\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"17657\">Ice Station Zebra</a> (1968) that prioritize cat-and-mouse tension over battle scenes."
      }
